---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import InflationCalculator from '@/components/tools/finance/InflationCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="inflation-calculator">
  <InflationCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the inflation calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Stay in <strong>US CPI data</strong> mode to compare two real years. Enter an amount, the
        year it applies to, and the year you want it expressed in.
      </li>
      <li>
        The comparison works in both directions. Set the second year earlier than the first to ask
        what today's money was worth back then.
      </li>
      <li>
        Switch to <strong>custom rate</strong> mode to project forward at a rate you choose. Use it for
        planning, and for countries outside the US.
      </li>
      <li>
        The year-by-year table shows the index, the annual inflation rate, and the value of your amount
        in each year between. Export it as CSV for a spreadsheet.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How the calculation works</h2>
    <p>
      Each result of the inflation calculator comes from one ratio:
      <code>Value = amount × CPI(to year) / CPI(from year)</code>. The Consumer Price Index measures
      the cost of a fixed basket of goods and services, so the ratio of two index values is the ratio
      of two price levels. Cumulative inflation is that ratio minus one. The average annual rate is the
      ratio to the power of one over the number of years, minus one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The data is the annual average CPI-U series from the US Bureau of Labor Statistics: Consumer Price
      Index for All Urban Consumers, US city average, all items, with the index base 1982-84 = 100. The
      complete series from 1913 is in this page, so the calculator works offline.
    </p>

    <h2>Average annual US inflation by decade</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Decade</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Average per year</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Cumulative</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>1920–1930</td><td style="text-align:right">−1.79%</td><td style="text-align:right">−17%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1930–1940</td><td style="text-align:right">−1.75%</td><td style="text-align:right">−16%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1940–1950</td><td style="text-align:right">5.58%</td><td style="text-align:right">+72%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1950–1960</td><td style="text-align:right">2.08%</td><td style="text-align:right">+23%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1960–1970</td><td style="text-align:right">2.74%</td><td style="text-align:right">+31%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1970–1980</td><td style="text-align:right">7.82%</td><td style="text-align:right">+112%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1980–1990</td><td style="text-align:right">4.72%</td><td style="text-align:right">+59%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1990–2000</td><td style="text-align:right">2.80%</td><td style="text-align:right">+32%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2000–2010</td><td style="text-align:right">2.39%</td><td style="text-align:right">+27%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2010–2020</td><td style="text-align:right">1.73%</td><td style="text-align:right">+19%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2020–2025</td><td style="text-align:right">4.50%</td><td style="text-align:right">+25%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The 1920s and 1930s were deflationary: prices fell. In the 1970s, prices more than doubled in a
      decade. The 2010s were the calmest period on record, which is part of why the 2021–2023 period
      felt so sharp.
    </p>

    <h2>What 100 from a past year is worth now</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[420px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Year</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Equivalent in 2025</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Average inflation since</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>1913</td><td style="text-align:right">3,258.59</td><td style="text-align:right">3.16%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1950</td><td style="text-align:right">1,338.59</td><td style="text-align:right">3.52%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1970</td><td style="text-align:right">831.44</td><td style="text-align:right">3.93%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1980</td><td style="text-align:right">391.50</td><td style="text-align:right">3.08%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1990</td><td style="text-align:right">246.82</td><td style="text-align:right">2.62%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2000</td><td style="text-align:right">187.34</td><td style="text-align:right">2.54%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2010</td><td style="text-align:right">147.94</td><td style="text-align:right">2.65%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2020</td><td style="text-align:right">124.65</td><td style="text-align:right">4.50%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Recent annual inflation</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[360px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Year</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">CPI-U annual average</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Inflation</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>2019</td><td style="text-align:right">255.657</td><td style="text-align:right">1.8%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2020</td><td style="text-align:right">258.811</td><td style="text-align:right">1.2%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2021</td><td style="text-align:right">270.970</td><td style="text-align:right">4.7%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2022</td><td style="text-align:right">292.655</td><td style="text-align:right">8.0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2023</td><td style="text-align:right">304.702</td><td style="text-align:right">4.1%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2024</td><td style="text-align:right">313.689</td><td style="text-align:right">2.9%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2025</td><td style="text-align:right">322.600</td><td style="text-align:right">2.8%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The 2025 annual average is provisional and may be revised by the BLS. Everything through 2024
      is a final published figure.
    </p>

    <h2>Why your personal inflation rate is different</h2>
    <p>
      CPI-U tracks one basket weighted to the spending of the average urban US household: about a third
      housing, an eighth food, a sixth transport, and so on. Your basket is different. Renters in a
      tight housing market, families who pay for childcare, and people with large medical or tuition
      costs have had higher inflation than the headline number for most of the past twenty years. A
      person with a fixed-rate mortgage and a short commute has had less.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are also method caveats. The index here uses annual averages, so a comparison between two
      years compares two twelve-month averages, not two specific dates. The BLS also applies hedonic
      quality adjustments and substitution assumptions. These are defensible, but the index is not a
      simple price tag comparison.
    </p>

    <h2>Using this outside the United States</h2>
    <p>
      The embedded table is US-only. For other countries, switch to custom rate mode and use the average
      inflation rate for the period from your national statistics office: the ONS in the UK, Eurostat or
      your national institute in the euro area, Statistics Canada, or the ABS in Australia. The
      arithmetic is the same. Only the index differs.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
